    {
      "block_id": "2605.24856_p1_b9",
      "text": "1. Introduction\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        508.97,
        162.5,
        520.93
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2035,
        650,
        2083
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.96,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Bold",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 15,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24856_p1_b10",
      "text": "The dominant paradigm in mechanistic interpretability extracts concept representations by identify-\ning the single \u201cbest layer\u201d \u2014 the residual stream depth at which a linear probe or difference-of-means\n(DoM) vector achieves maximum class separation [Zou et al., 2023; Arditi et al., 2024]. This heuristic\n",
      "bbox": [
        70.72,
        530.15,
        542.05,
        568.16
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        282,
        2120,
        2168,
        2272
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.8,
      "font_name": "LMRoman10-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 305,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24856_p1_b11",
      "text": "is computationally convenient and empirically grounded. It is also, by design, a snapshot: it identifies\nthe peak of a process rather than characterizing the process itself.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        570.8,
        540.2,
        595.26
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2283,
        2160,
        2381
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.8,
      "font_name": "LMRoman10-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 173,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24856_p1_b12",
      "text": "Transformers are iterative dynamical systems. Each layer applies a sequence of attention and MLP\noperations that write new information into the residual stream, modifying and extending what prior\nlayers contributed [Elhage et al., 2021]. A concept observed at Layer 15 was shaped by Layers 10\nthrough 14 before it; the best-layer heuristic tells us where the concept is most legible, not how it\narrived there.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.61,
        604.67,
        540.49,
        669.78
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        2418,
        2161,
        2679
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman10-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 409,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24856_p1_b13",
      "text": "This paper introduces the Concept Allocation Zone (CAZ) framework, which extends the\ninterpretability toolkit from anatomy \u2014 where is the concept most visible? \u2014 to dynamical flow\n\u2014 how does the concept form? The CAZ is defined as a region of model depth allocated to the\n",
      "bbox": [
